# Deployment data never lives in this tree — it belongs to $LOC_HOME. That is
# an expectation about the environment, so the tree enforces it as well: one
# LOC_HOME unset, or pointed here, would otherwise put run-time state in a
# repository. `*.log` alone was partial cover — it catches delivery.log and
# misses the message log's <date>.jsonl, which is the file that holds message
# bodies in plain text. The whole directory is ignored instead, so `git add -A`
# cannot stage it; check-clean.sh reads the working tree rather than the index,
# so anything present is still reported.
*.log
/run/
.DS_Store

# The Go build tree. Binaries are artifacts, not source.
/build/

# Dependencies come from the module proxy, verified against go.sum.
/vendor/

# Editor and IDE state. It belongs to a person's machine and carries that
# machine's absolute paths, which is the one thing check-clean.sh exists to
# keep out of a tree written for strangers. Ignored so it cannot be swept in
# by `git add -A`; check-clean.sh reads the working tree rather than the
# index, so a directory that is present is still reported by a hand run.
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
